## Networking Concepts

### OSI Model vs TCP/IP Model

| OSI Model | TCP/IP Model |
|-----------|-------------|
| Physical | Link |
| Data Link | Link |
| Network | Internet |
| Transport | Transport |
| Session | Application |
| Presentation | Application |
| Application | Application |

### Where Common Protocols Fit

- **IP** → Internet Layer
- **TCP/UDP** → Transport Layer
- **HTTP/HTTPS** → Application Layer
- **DNS** → Application Layer

### Real Example

`curl https://google.com` works through:

Application (HTTP) → Transport (TCP) → Internet (IP)

---

## Task 1 – Check Network Identity

### Command

```bash
hostname -I
```

### Output

```text
172.31.33.72 172.17.0.1
```

### Observation

- `172.31.33.72` is my EC2 private IP.
- `172.17.0.1` belongs to Docker's bridge network.

---

## Task 2 – Test Connectivity

### Command

```bash
ping -c 4 google.com
```

### Result

```text
4 packets transmitted, 4 received, 0% packet loss
Average latency: ~2.97 ms
```

### Observation

The server successfully reached Google with very low latency and no packet loss.

---

## Task 3 – Trace the Network Path

### Command

```bash
tracepath google.com
```

### Observation

- Initial AWS network hops responded successfully.
- Several later hops showed "no reply", which is common because many routers block ICMP responses.
- The path MTU settled at **1500 bytes**.

---

## Task 4 – Check Listening Ports

### Command

```bash
ss -tulpn
```

### Important Listening Services

| Port | Service |
|------|---------|
| 22 | SSH |
| 80 | Nginx |
| 53 | Local DNS |
| 68 | DHCP |

### Observation

- SSH is listening on port **22**.
- Nginx is listening on port **80**.
- DNS services are running locally on port **53**.

---

## Task 5 – DNS Resolution

### Command

```bash
getent hosts google.com
```

### Output

```text
2a00:1450:400f:806::200e google.com
```

### Observation

The system successfully resolved Google's IPv6 address.

---

## Task 6 – HTTP Check

### Command

```bash
curl -I https://google.com
```

### Output

```text
HTTP/2 301
```

### Observation

Google redirected the request to `www.google.com`, which confirms HTTPS connectivity is working.

---

## Task 7 – Local Web Server Check

### Command

```bash
curl -I http://localhost
```

### Output

```text
HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Server: nginx/1.28.3 (Ubuntu)
```

### Observation

My local Nginx server responded successfully with **HTTP 200 OK**.

